          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            various concepts of “renormalization groups” describe the choices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
            and their behaviour under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+transformations"} scaling transformations
            or choices of cutoffs.
          %p
            There are at least three different concepts referred to as “the renormalization group”, only the first is in general really a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
                (
                %a{:href => "#StueckelbergPetermann53"}> Stückelberg-Petermann 53
                , historically the origin of the concept)
              %p
                this is literally the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em re-normalizations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} normalization schemes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}'
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/precomposition"} precomposition
                with a transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒵
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Z}
                of the space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"}> renormalization group flow
                , say along
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+transformations"} scaling transformations
                yielding the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gell-Mann-Low+renormalization+cocycle"} Gell-Mann-Low renormalization cocycle
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilsonian+RG"} Wilsonian RG
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theories"} effective quantum field theories
                defined with a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09"} Brunetti-Dütsch-Fredenhagen 09, p. 10
          %p In more detail:
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vac
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mtext BV-BRST
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mi kin
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  vac
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}_{kin}, \Delta_H )
          %p
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            (according to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "S-matrix#VacuumFree"} this def.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theory"} interacting
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
          %p
            Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative S-matrix
            scheme/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} ("re"-)normalization scheme
            around this vacuum (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}> this def.
            ) is a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LocObs
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mtext BV-BRST
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
